elevant because it helps explain why RCC is categorized as a kidney cancer and how it relates to kidney structures involved in filtration and urine formation. [Data: Entities (2821, 2840, 2860)]\"},{\"summary\":\"RCC subtype classification is explicitly modeled and indicated as clinically important\",\"explanation\":\"The dataset includes an explicit concept of “RCC subtypes,” described as the classification of renal cell carcinoma into subtypes. This indicates that the community captures not just the existence of RCC but also its internal taxonomy. [Data: Relationships (5204)]\\n\\nSubtype classification is described as clinically important because it can influence treatment decisions. While the data does not specify which treatments, it clearly states that subtype differentiation has downstream clinical impact, making classification a key decision-support component. [Data: Entities (2821); Relationships (5204)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Clear cell RCC (ccRCC) is identified as the most common RCC subtype\",\"explanation\":\"Clear cell RCC is explicitly described as a subtype of RCC and the most common subtype, accounting for about 7 out of 10 RCC cases. This makes ccRCC a high-priority subtype within the community’s disease taxonomy. [Data: Entities (2864); Relationships (5308)]\\n\\nThe subtype is characterized by pale/clear-appearing cells under the microscope, indicating that morphology (histologic appearance) is part of how RCC subtypes are distinguished in this community. [Data: Entities (2864, 2821)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Unclassified RCC captures diagnostic complexity when tumors don’t fit known subtypes\",\"explanation\":\"Unclassified RCC is described as a category used when tumor cells do not match known subtypes or when more than one subtype is present. This suggests that RCC classification can encounter ambiguous or mixed-pattern cases requiring a catch-all diagnostic label. [Data: Entities (2869); Relationships (5310)]\\n\\nThe explicit linkage of RCC to unclassif